## mandarin-float

mandarin-float-ask = Why does a mandarin in its peel not sink?
mandarin-float-title = A mandarin in water
mandarin-float-choice-peel = The peel is light and spongy, with air in it: with it the mandarin is lighter than the same volume of water
mandarin-float-choice-flesh = The flesh itself is lighter than water; the peel has nothing to do with it
mandarin-float-choice-shape = Its round shape and smooth peel hold it up — the water will not let it go under
mandarin-float-diameter-label = Mandarin’s diameter
mandarin-float-peel-label = Peel’s thickness
mandarin-float-salt-label = Salt to 1 L of water
mandarin-float-setup = A mandarin, a balance, a beaker of water, a thin rod and a thread; salt
mandarin-float-question = A mandarin { $d } across, its peel { $peel }. We weigh it, put it into a beaker of water on the balance and push it under with a rod: the balance shows how much water it pushes aside. Then we peel it and test the flesh and the peel the same way. And last — the peeled mandarin into water with { $salt } of salt dissolved per litre. Why does a mandarin in its peel not sink?
mandarin-float-optional-salt = What if the water is salty?
